            <ArticleTitle>[A giant non-parasitic liver cyst as a prominent epigastric mass in an obese patient].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2316-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-0032-1327248</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY AND ADMISSION FINDINGS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 62-year-old, obese male patient was referred because of an increasing prominent epigastric mass and bloated feeling. An epigastric hernial defect was clinically excluded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Abdominal imaging by ultrasound and CT-scan demonstrated a giant liver cyst in the left lobe of 20 cm in diameter with a displacement of the stomach. Endoscopy showed an external compression of the stomach. Cystic echinococcosis was excluded by serology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT AND COURSE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic deroofing of the cyst resulted in immediate freedom of symptoms. The further course was uneventful. The patient was discharged one day after the operation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Liver cysts may become symptomatic by intestinal compression syndromes or - occasionally - as a prominent abdominal mass. Laparoscopic deroofing is the golden standard of symptomatic non parasitic liver cysts and is associated with a good clinical outcome.</AbstractText>
